<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<domain-models xmlns="http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models
    http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models/domain-models_7.4.xsd">

  <module name="aiaccountant" package="com.axelor.apps.aiaccountant.db"/>

  <!-- A local copy of one Azure Monitor datapoint for the Foundry resource.

    WHY IT EXISTS. Azure Monitor is the authority on what the resource actually served — it counts
    every request, including anything that never went through this application at all — while
    AiUsageStat is the only source for the breakdown Azure cannot give (which agent, which caller,
    which company, which outcome). The Stats page shows both, side by side, and neither is derived
    from the other. Copying the datapoints locally is what lets ordinary Axelor charts render them:
    a <chart> reads JPQL, it cannot call a REST API, and embedding the portal's own charts was never
    an option.

    ONE CANONICAL SERIES PER GRAIN — WHY THE UNIQUE CONSTRAINT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. The obvious
    implementation, asking Azure for 24h and 7d and 30d on every poll and inserting what comes back,
    silently triples every bucket that appears in more than one window and every chart built on it
    reads high. So there are at most two series per metric — hourly for the recent view, daily for the
    longer ones — each identified by (resource, metric, aggregation, grain, bucket, dimensions), and a
    poll UPSERTS that identity rather than inserting. A dashboard window is then a WHERE clause over
    one series, never a stored copy of its own. Charts must always constrain `intervalGrain`;
    selecting across grains sums the same hour twice, once from each series.

    WHY THE ROLLING RE-FETCH. Each poll re-reads its whole lookback window rather than only what is
    new, because Azure revises recent buckets after the fact. Re-reading and overwriting is what makes
    a late correction land; asking only for new buckets would freeze the first, incomplete figure.

    WHY THE VALUE IS DECIMAL. Token and request counters are whole numbers, but latency, rates and
    every Average aggregation are not. A long would silently truncate them.

    WHY dimensionKey EXISTS BESIDE THE NAMED COLUMNS. Azure's dimension set differs per metric, so a
    fixed column per dimension is either wrong for some metric or a schema change away from being
    wrong. dimensionKey is a canonical, deterministically ordered encoding of the whole tuple and
    exists so the unique identity is complete whatever the dimensions turn out to be;
    modelDeploymentName and modelName are stored again, on their own, only so charts can group by them
    without parsing a string. -->
  <entity name="AiAzureMetricSnapshot">

    <!-- The ARM resource id the datapoint was read from. Part of the identity so pointing the module
      at a second Foundry resource cannot collide with what was already collected from the first. -->
    <string name="resourceId" title="Resource ID" required="true" max="512"/>

    <string name="metricName" title="Metric" required="true" max="128" index="true"/>

    <!-- `Total`, `Average`, `Count`, … The same metric under two aggregations is two series, not one
      value measured twice. -->
    <string name="aggregation" title="Aggregation" required="true" max="32"/>

    <!-- ISO 8601 duration: `PT1H` or `P1D`. Part of the identity, and the column every chart must
      filter on. -->
    <string name="intervalGrain" title="Grain" required="true" max="16" index="true"/>

    <!-- Start of the bucket, UTC, as Azure reported it. -->
    <datetime name="bucketTimestamp" title="Bucket" required="true" index="true"/>

    <!-- Canonical encoding of the full dimension tuple, e.g.
      `ModelDeploymentName=gpt-4o|ModelName=gpt-4o-2024-08-06`. Empty string, never null, when the
      metric has no dimensions — a null here would make the unique constraint stop constraining,
      since two nulls do not conflict in SQL. -->
    <string name="dimensionKey" title="Dimensions" required="true" max="512" default=""/>

    <string name="modelDeploymentName" title="Deployment" max="128"/>
    <string name="modelName" title="Model" max="128"/>

    <string name="unit" title="Unit" max="32"/>

    <decimal name="metricValue" title="Value" precision="20" scale="6" default="0"/>

    <!-- When this row was last written from Azure. Read by the Stats page to say how fresh the panel
      is, which is the difference between "the assistant was quiet" and "the poll has been failing". -->
    <datetime name="collectedDateTime" title="Collected"/>

    <unique-constraint
      columns="resourceId,metricName,aggregation,intervalGrain,bucketTimestamp,dimensionKey"/>

  </entity>

</domain-models>
